1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,088
The release app ran once on haiku, and
failed: no skill loaded, and an entry for

2
00:00:05,088 --> 00:00:05,850
the refactor.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:10,175
Vally's own trials of that skill passed
three of eight on haiku.

4
00:00:10,675 --> 00:00:15,532
So is the app worse than those trials, or
was one run unlucky, and does it cost the

5
00:00:15,532 --> 00:00:15,825
same?

6
00:00:16,325 --> 00:00:20,671
One run can't say, and a run of the app
that has already happened can't be

7
00:00:20,671 --> 00:00:21,200
repeated.

8
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:26,252
Here, you'll plug the app's own code into
Vally, so it runs as many trials as we

9
00:00:26,252 --> 00:00:29,325
like, on any model, under the graders we
already have.

10
00:00:30,533 --> 00:00:33,808
Let's start with where an app could fit
inside Vally.

11
00:00:34,308 --> 00:00:38,430
Around every trial, Vally does the same
work: it prepares the workspace, and

12
00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:41,033
afterwards saves the patch and runs the
graders.

13
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:45,808
One step in the middle runs the agent, and
that step is the executor.

14
00:00:46,308 --> 00:00:50,608
The executor gets a stimulus and a
workspace, and hands back a trajectory:

15
00:00:50,608 --> 00:00:52,583
the account of what the agent did.

16
00:00:53,083 --> 00:00:55,733
That's the one part we're about to swap.

17
00:00:56,933 --> 00:01:02,408
Vally's own executor opens a Copilot SDK
session, with settings that Vally chose.

18
00:01:02,908 --> 00:01:06,958
The release app opens a session too, with
settings the team chose.

19
00:01:07,458 --> 00:01:10,608
Those choices are part of the feature
being tested.

20
00:01:11,108 --> 00:01:15,958
If the app's agent behaves differently, an
eval through Vally's executor won't show

21
00:01:15,958 --> 00:01:16,133
it.

22
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:21,420
Once the executor is one that calls the
app, every trial runs the code the team

23
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:21,783
ships.

24
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,723
That gives us three ways to look at the
release feature, and each answers

25
00:01:26,723 --> 00:01:28,100
something the others can't.

26
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:33,293
Vally's own executor shows whether the
skill does its job, on any model, apart

27
00:01:33,293 --> 00:01:34,075
from the app.

28
00:01:34,575 --> 00:01:38,874
Running the app through an executor shows
whether the feature, as shipped, does its

29
00:01:38,874 --> 00:01:41,050
job, and it's repeatable, so we can count.

30
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:46,202
Grading the app's real runs afterwards
shows whether it's still doing its job in

31
00:01:46,202 --> 00:01:46,725
real use.

32
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:51,158
That's the idea; here's the file that
expresses it.

33
00:01:51,658 --> 00:01:56,108
An executor plugin is a JavaScript module,
and the starter ships this one with

34
00:01:56,108 --> 00:01:58,333
everything written except one function.

35
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:02,558
Our executor is an object, and its name is
release app.

36
00:02:03,058 --> 00:02:07,333
Its execute function runs one trial, and
it's the part still to write.

37
00:02:07,833 --> 00:02:12,199
Shutdown is called at the end, and has
nothing to clean up, because the app opens

38
00:02:12,199 --> 00:02:14,408
and closes its own session in each trial.

39
00:02:14,908 --> 00:02:19,652
Vally looks for one exported function,
register executors, and calls it with its

40
00:02:19,652 --> 00:02:22,083
registry: the list of executors it knows.

41
00:02:22,583 --> 00:02:27,385
Ours adds this object to that list, so its
name is what a command or an eval file can

42
00:02:27,385 --> 00:02:29,983
ask for, the way they ask for a model by
name.

43
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:33,400
Here's that function, written.

44
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:38,400
Vally hands it two things: the stimulus,
and the options for this one trial.

45
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:43,074
The function passes them on to write
entries, which is the app's own function:

46
00:02:43,074 --> 00:02:45,000
the one the app's run script called.

47
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,987
Where to work is the first option: the
prepared workspace, after the setup

48
00:02:49,987 --> 00:02:50,775
commands ran.

49
00:02:51,275 --> 00:02:55,525
Which model to run comes next, so one
command can try any model.

50
00:02:56,025 --> 00:03:00,854
Skills arrive as the path of each skill
file, and the SDK wants folders, so each

51
00:03:00,854 --> 00:03:02,725
path is cut back to its folder.

52
00:03:03,225 --> 00:03:07,400
The request is the stimulus's prompt, and
the time limit is Vally's.

53
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:12,550
For traces, the app gets a file in the
trial's results folder to write them to.

54
00:03:13,050 --> 00:03:16,625
Last is the trial's trace context, which
we'll come to.

55
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:22,612
What comes back from the app is the
agent's reply, and every session event the

56
00:03:22,612 --> 00:03:23,408
SDK reported.

57
00:03:23,908 --> 00:03:27,908
One helper in the same file renames those
events to Vally's.

58
00:03:28,408 --> 00:03:32,730
Then the function returns a trajectory:
the events, the reply as the trial's

59
00:03:32,730 --> 00:03:34,208
output, and the workspace.

60
00:03:34,708 --> 00:03:39,504
Its metrics come from compute metrics, a
function Vally exports that adds up a

61
00:03:39,504 --> 00:03:40,733
trajectory's events.

62
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:46,001
That way, a plugin's tokens, cost and tool
calls are counted the way the built-in

63
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:46,883
executor's are.

64
00:03:47,383 --> 00:03:51,867
Compute metrics can't know how long the
app took, so the wall time is ours to

65
00:03:51,867 --> 00:03:52,333
measure.

66
00:03:52,833 --> 00:03:57,536
Last comes some metadata: the model, the
skills that loaded, and the executor's

67
00:03:57,536 --> 00:03:57,833
name.

68
00:03:59,033 --> 00:04:03,733
That renaming helper is short, and three
of its branches matter to our graders.

69
00:04:04,233 --> 00:04:08,983
When the SDK reports a tool starting, the
helper adds a tool call event: the kind

70
00:04:08,983 --> 00:04:10,683
our tool-calls graders check.

71
00:04:11,183 --> 00:04:15,730
When a skill is opened, it adds a skill
activation, the event that says a skill

72
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:16,133
loaded.

73
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:21,433
Both the skill-invocation grader and the
report's Skills column rely on that one.

74
00:04:21,933 --> 00:04:26,367
After each model call, it adds a token
usage event: how many tokens the call

75
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:28,058
used, with its cost attached.

76
00:04:28,558 --> 00:04:33,076
So cost is filled in for these trials,
which it wasn't when we graded the app's

77
00:04:33,076 --> 00:04:33,933
run afterwards.

78
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:39,984
Vally loads the plugin from dot vally dot
yaml, the settings file that holds our

79
00:04:39,984 --> 00:04:40,408
suites.

80
00:04:40,908 --> 00:04:45,808
Three new lines at its top list the plugin
file, under plugins, as an executor.

81
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:50,058
Loading the file doesn't select the
executor.

82
00:04:50,558 --> 00:04:55,296
We choose it for one run with dash dash
executor, for an eval file under defaults,

83
00:04:55,296 --> 00:04:57,433
or for an experiment under overrides.

84
00:04:58,633 --> 00:05:02,608
One more thing an executor does is declare
what it supports.

85
00:05:03,108 --> 00:05:07,858
Ours declares nothing, so it can't run a
stimulus that has a simulation block.

86
00:05:08,358 --> 00:05:12,854
And our release eval has one: tracker
down, where a tool is made to fail on

87
00:05:12,854 --> 00:05:13,333
purpose.

88
00:05:13,833 --> 00:05:18,432
Vally checks before starting the agent, so
through this executor, that stimulus ends

89
00:05:18,432 --> 00:05:21,333
in error, with this message, and spends no
agent run.

90
00:05:21,833 --> 00:05:26,195
That error will show in every run that
follows, and it's expected: it isn't a

91
00:05:26,195 --> 00:05:27,158
fault in the app.

92
00:05:28,367 --> 00:05:32,367
Both programs keep traces, and so far
nothing has linked them.

93
00:05:32,867 --> 00:05:37,182
Vally writes spans of its own for every
trial, to a file in the run folder: one

94
00:05:37,182 --> 00:05:40,242
span for the trial, and inside it, one for
each attempt.

95
00:05:40,742 --> 00:05:45,218
Our app writes its spans to its own file,
and the outermost, named invoke agent,

96
00:05:45,218 --> 00:05:46,617
covers one whole session.

97
00:05:47,117 --> 00:05:50,817
When the app runs alone, that session span
has no parent.

98
00:05:51,317 --> 00:05:56,009
If both send their spans to one tracing
tool, we'd want the app's spans inside the

99
00:05:56,009 --> 00:05:56,467
trial's.

100
00:05:56,967 --> 00:06:01,285
Then a slow or expensive trial can be
opened, and followed from Vally's step

101
00:06:01,285 --> 00:06:02,592
down to the model call.

102
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:07,375
What links two programs' spans is a trace
context.

103
00:06:07,875 --> 00:06:12,455
It's the ID of a trace, and of the span
that's open in it, passed from one program

104
00:06:12,455 --> 00:06:13,125
to the next.

105
00:06:13,625 --> 00:06:18,150
With it, the second program's spans join
the first one's trace, as children.

106
00:06:18,650 --> 00:06:23,337
One web standard writes it as a short
string named trace parent: a version, the

107
00:06:23,337 --> 00:06:26,125
trace ID, the parent span's ID, and some
flags.

108
00:06:27,333 --> 00:06:31,900
Vally passes each trial's trace context to
the executor, in its options, and our

109
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:34,183
execute function hands it on to the app.

110
00:06:34,683 --> 00:06:39,095
The Copilot SDK has a hook for exactly
this: a function it calls to ask which

111
00:06:39,095 --> 00:06:42,533
trace to join, before it opens a session
or sends a message.

112
00:06:43,033 --> 00:06:48,342
So the app needed two lines: give the SDK
whatever trace context the caller gave, or

113
00:06:48,342 --> 00:06:48,658
none.

114
00:06:49,158 --> 00:06:53,172
When the app runs from its own script,
there's none, and the session starts a

115
00:06:53,172 --> 00:06:54,058
trace of its own.

116
00:06:54,558 --> 00:06:58,668
When it runs from our executor, the
session span should carry the trial's

117
00:06:58,668 --> 00:07:01,483
trace ID, with Vally's attempt span as its
parent.

118
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:05,700
That's how the plugin works; now let's run
it.

119
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:10,225
First, the release eval goes through the
app once on each model.

120
00:07:10,725 --> 00:07:15,400
Then an experiment runs it three more
times on each: four trials a model in all.

121
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:20,250
In its one earlier run of the app, haiku
failed, without loading the skill.

122
00:07:20,750 --> 00:07:25,300
How many of four would you expect it to
pass now, and would terra do any better?

123
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:30,675
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

124
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:36,574
The execute function is now in the plugin
file, and the three plugins lines are in

125
00:07:36,574 --> 00:07:37,600
the settings file.

126
00:07:38,100 --> 00:07:42,374
First, node dash dash check tells us
whether the file is valid JavaScript,

127
00:07:42,374 --> 00:07:43,875
without starting anything.

128
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:50,350
It prints nothing, which means no errors.

129
00:07:50,850 --> 00:07:55,503
Now the release eval, with dash dash
executor release app, on both models, two

130
00:07:55,503 --> 00:07:56,100
at a time.

131
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:01,725
It spends two agent runs, because tracker
down is refused before any agent starts.

132
00:08:02,225 --> 00:08:07,032
The table at the end counts stimuli, two
for each model, and the refused one can't

133
00:08:07,032 --> 00:08:07,325
pass.

134
00:08:09,633 --> 00:08:14,158
Moments later, haiku has failed since last
release, and terra has passed it.

135
00:08:17,658 --> 00:08:21,833
So the counts are none of two stimuli for
haiku, and one of two for terra.

136
00:08:27,033 --> 00:08:31,627
The report in the new results folder
confirms what ran: under each table, the

137
00:08:31,627 --> 00:08:33,058
executor is release app.

138
00:08:33,558 --> 00:08:38,132
Haiku's table has no Skills column at all,
because no skill loaded, and its tool

139
00:08:38,132 --> 00:08:40,133
calls were four views and one edit.

140
00:08:40,633 --> 00:08:45,058
Terra's lists the changelog entry skill,
and a skill call among its tools.

141
00:08:48,267 --> 00:08:52,818
The app's traces for a trial are kept in
that trial's results folder, and Vally's

142
00:08:52,818 --> 00:08:54,392
spans are in the run folder.

143
00:08:54,892 --> 00:08:59,042
Here's one line from each, for haiku's
trial, cut to three fields.

144
00:08:59,542 --> 00:09:03,892
Both carry one trace ID, so the app's
session joined Vally's trace.

145
00:09:04,392 --> 00:09:09,233
Its parent is Vally's attempt span, so
every model call and tool call beneath it

146
00:09:09,233 --> 00:09:09,717
follows.

147
00:09:12,933 --> 00:09:17,324
For more trials, we use an experiment
file, the kind we've used to compare

148
00:09:17,324 --> 00:09:17,858
variants.

149
00:09:18,358 --> 00:09:22,561
It names the release eval, and two
variants, haiku and terra, which differ

150
00:09:22,561 --> 00:09:23,583
only in the model.

151
00:09:24,083 --> 00:09:29,117
What's new is one line under the top-level
overrides: executor, release app, set once

152
00:09:29,117 --> 00:09:30,183
for both variants.

153
00:09:30,683 --> 00:09:32,908
Beside it, runs is three.

154
00:09:34,133 --> 00:09:38,583
With dash dash dry run, Vally plans the
experiment and starts nothing.

155
00:09:43,753 --> 00:09:48,103
It finds two plans, one for each variant,
with haiku as the baseline.

156
00:09:53,333 --> 00:09:57,433
Now the real thing: three trials a model,
through the app, two at a time.

157
00:09:57,933 --> 00:10:02,706
That's six agent runs, since tracker down
is refused again, three times for each

158
00:10:02,706 --> 00:10:03,183
variant.

159
00:10:07,387 --> 00:10:12,112
About a minute later, the table gives
terra one stimulus of two, and haiku none.

160
00:10:16,612 --> 00:10:20,537
Under that, terra used about a quarter
fewer tokens than haiku.

161
00:10:24,767 --> 00:10:29,162
That table counts stimuli, so we count
three more things from each variant's

162
00:10:29,162 --> 00:10:29,567
trials.

163
00:10:30,067 --> 00:10:34,314
Haiku passed none of three, loaded the
skill in one, and wrote an entry for the

164
00:10:34,314 --> 00:10:36,842
refactor, pull request seventy-six, every
time.

165
00:10:37,342 --> 00:10:42,017
Terra passed all three, loaded the skill
in all three, and left the refactor out.

166
00:10:42,517 --> 00:10:47,277
Adding up each trial's cost, haiku's three
came to about six point seven AIU, and

167
00:10:47,277 --> 00:10:49,217
terra's to about ten point seven.

168
00:10:52,433 --> 00:10:54,408
So what can we measure now?

169
00:10:54,908 --> 00:10:59,405
The same eval file ran unchanged: its
setup command, its hidden test and its

170
00:10:59,405 --> 00:11:02,658
tool-calls graders didn't depend on which
executor ran.

171
00:11:03,158 --> 00:11:07,958
Cost was filled in again, because the
executor reports the runtime's own usage.

172
00:11:08,458 --> 00:11:13,295
Budgets, ranges and comparisons all work
on these trials, because they're ordinary

173
00:11:13,295 --> 00:11:13,708
trials.

174
00:11:14,933 --> 00:11:17,508
Now the earlier question has counts.

175
00:11:18,008 --> 00:11:22,363
Earlier, our runs of this stimulus went
through Vally's own executor, eight trials

176
00:11:22,363 --> 00:11:25,283
a model, and the app has now run it four
times on each.

177
00:11:25,783 --> 00:11:28,458
Passed is each run's own verdict.

178
00:11:28,958 --> 00:11:33,958
Tokens a model call is a row's trial
tokens, divided by its model calls.

179
00:11:34,458 --> 00:11:38,408
AIU a trial is a row's cost, divided by
its trials.

180
00:11:38,908 --> 00:11:43,233
Two things stand out: one is a weak sign,
and one held in every trial.

181
00:11:46,433 --> 00:11:51,108
First, the weak sign: how the app behaves,
beside Vally's own runs of the skill.

182
00:11:51,608 --> 00:11:55,158
Terra loaded the skill and passed every
time, either way.

183
00:11:55,658 --> 00:11:57,858
Haiku is where they differ.

184
00:11:58,358 --> 00:12:02,569
Through the app, it loaded the skill in
one of five trials, counting its one

185
00:12:02,569 --> 00:12:03,233
earlier run.

186
00:12:03,733 --> 00:12:07,108
Through Vally's executor, it loaded in
four of eight.

187
00:12:07,608 --> 00:12:11,377
As ranges, that's four to sixty-two
percent, against twenty-two to

188
00:12:11,377 --> 00:12:13,433
seventy-eight, which overlap widely.

189
00:12:13,933 --> 00:12:18,243
In an earlier run of ours, the two were
closer: none of five, against one of

190
00:12:18,243 --> 00:12:18,583
eight.

191
00:12:19,083 --> 00:12:23,605
So with this few trials, we can't yet say
that the app makes haiku worse, but now we

192
00:12:23,605 --> 00:12:25,058
can run more, and find out.

193
00:12:26,267 --> 00:12:30,842
Second, the one that held in every trial:
the app costs more per model call.

194
00:12:31,342 --> 00:12:35,918
The test is a strict one: even the app's
lowest trial, against the highest through

195
00:12:35,918 --> 00:12:36,867
Vally's executor.

196
00:12:37,367 --> 00:12:41,521
On haiku, the app's lowest used about
seventeen thousand four hundred tokens a

197
00:12:41,521 --> 00:12:45,516
call, and the highest through Vally's
executor about fifteen thousand three

198
00:12:45,516 --> 00:12:45,942
hundred.

199
00:12:46,442 --> 00:12:50,842
On terra, it was about fourteen thousand,
against twelve thousand five hundred.

200
00:12:51,342 --> 00:12:56,168
On average, that's about sixteen percent
more a call on haiku, and thirteen percent

201
00:12:56,168 --> 00:12:56,692
on terra.

202
00:12:59,900 --> 00:13:03,675
Why would one model need more tokens for
each call through the app?

203
00:13:04,175 --> 00:13:07,825
The app's traces show the reason: an MCP
server.

204
00:13:08,325 --> 00:13:12,975
That's a program that offers an agent
extra tools, through a shared protocol.

205
00:13:13,475 --> 00:13:18,394
Our app's sessions include GitHub's hosted
one, which adds five GitHub tools to every

206
00:13:18,394 --> 00:13:19,725
request the model sees.

207
00:13:20,225 --> 00:13:23,450
Vally's own executor turns that server
off.

208
00:13:24,667 --> 00:13:29,102
More tokens a call doesn't have to mean a
more expensive trial, because a trial's

209
00:13:29,102 --> 00:13:31,567
cost also depends on how many calls it
makes.

210
00:13:32,067 --> 00:13:36,439
On haiku, the app's trials made fewer
calls: five and a quarter a trial, against

211
00:13:36,439 --> 00:13:39,117
five and three quarters through Vally's
executor.

212
00:13:39,617 --> 00:13:44,291
So they cost about the same: two point
five four AIU a trial, against two point

213
00:13:44,291 --> 00:13:44,942
five eight.

214
00:13:45,442 --> 00:13:50,152
On terra, the app's trials made slightly
more calls, and cost more: about four AIU

215
00:13:50,152 --> 00:13:52,392
a trial, against three point two seven.

216
00:13:52,892 --> 00:13:56,342
Each call through the app carries the same
extra cost.

217
00:13:56,842 --> 00:14:01,217
Whether a whole trial costs more depends
on how many calls that trial makes.

218
00:14:01,717 --> 00:14:05,567
An eval of the skill alone wouldn't have
shown that overhead at all.

219
00:14:06,767 --> 00:14:11,208
Earlier, this table could only say what
running the app through an executor might

220
00:14:11,208 --> 00:14:11,592
answer.

221
00:14:12,092 --> 00:14:16,829
Now it has answered: on terra, the app did
as well as the skill alone; on haiku, it's

222
00:14:16,829 --> 00:14:20,117
too early to say; and every call paid for
five extra tools.

223
00:14:20,617 --> 00:14:25,131
Neither could be counted from the app's
real runs, and the second was invisible

224
00:14:25,131 --> 00:14:27,017
from inside Vally's own executor.

225
00:14:27,517 --> 00:14:32,117
That's the reason to plug the app in: the
eval now measures what the team ships.

226
00:14:33,333 --> 00:14:37,879
Before you go on: what does an executor
plugin export, and what does its execute

227
00:14:37,879 --> 00:14:38,958
function give back?

228
00:14:41,958 --> 00:14:46,608
It exports register executors, which
registers an executor by name.

229
00:14:47,108 --> 00:14:52,158
Its execute function receives the stimulus
and the trial's options, and runs the app.

230
00:14:52,658 --> 00:14:57,008
It returns a trajectory: the events, the
agent's reply, and the metrics.

231
00:14:58,233 --> 00:15:02,824
You can now run the app's own agent code
under Vally's trials, experiments and

232
00:15:02,824 --> 00:15:03,883
model comparisons.

233
00:15:04,383 --> 00:15:06,683
With that, the course is complete.

234
00:15:07,183 --> 00:15:11,331
The workflow guide walks the whole
procedure, for a skill or for a feature in

235
00:15:11,331 --> 00:15:11,708
an app.

236
00:15:12,208 --> 00:15:15,783
The exercises on the lesson page are worth
doing, too.
